WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.400
<v Instructor>So let's summarize the kind of ways</v>

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.690
we can protect your network

3
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.210
on AWS that we know about so far.

4
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.880
So we've seen network access control list,

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.730
we've seen Amazon VPC security groups,

6
00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.770
we've seen AWS WAF to protect

7
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.360
against malicious requests made

8
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.900
through HTTP on specific services that we have

9
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.750
we've seen AWS Shield and Shield Advance to protect

10
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.110
against DDoS, and we've seen AWS Firewall Manager

11
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.930
to manage them all your rules for WAF

12
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.170
and Shield and so on across multiple accounts.

13
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.150
Okay, so we know about this, but what

14
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.170
if you want to protect

15
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.040
our entire VPC in a sophisticated way

16
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.850
Introducing the AWS Network Firewall.

17
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.120
So this is used to protect your entire VPC with a Firewall.

18
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.590
So here's my VPC

19
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.200
and the Firewall is going to be all around it.

20
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.512
And so this AWS Network Firewall protects it

21
00:00:51.512 --> 00:00:53.070
from the layer three

22
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.350
to the layer seven protection.

23
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.800
So you can inspect any kind of traffic in any direction.

24
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.770
So you can expect VPC to VPC traffic.

25
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.620
outbound to internet, inbound from internet,

26
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.360
and to and from Direct Connect

27
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.310
and Site-to-Site VPN connections.

28
00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.420
So I represent that in there.

29
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.760
So here we protect anything coming in

30
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.940
and out of the internet, in and out of a peer VPC

31
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.620
and in and out of Direct Connect

32
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.170
or a Site-to-Site VPN connection.

33
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.910
So we define rules

34
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.250
and then we can control anything that happens.

35
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.680
So internally, the Network Firewall

36
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.560
uses the AWS Gateway Load Balancer,

37
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.820
but instead of us setting

38
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.420
up a third party appliance to inspect the traffic,

39
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.470
we just have AWS manage it with its own appliances

40
00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.210
and so therefore we have our own rules

41
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.770
on the Network Firewall.

42
00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.530
And these rules can be centrally managed as well

43
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.380
across multiple accounts and many VPCs

44
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.980
on the AWS Firewall Manager Service.

45
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.180
So with the Network Firewall,

46
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.410
we have fine grained controls

47
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.800
over all kind of network traffic.

48
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.800
So we support thousands of rules at our VPC level.

49
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.080
We can filter by IP and by port

50
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.090
with tens of thousands of IPs.

51
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.860
We can filter by protocol.

52
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.470
For example, we can disable the SMB protocol

53
00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.120
for outbound communication.

54
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.490
We can filter at the domain level.

55
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.230
So we say, "Hey, you only allow

56
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.910
outbound traffic from our VPC

57
00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.600
into our My corporate domain or any third party

58
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.000
software repository that we only allow."

59
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.700
We can do general pattern matching with regex and so on,

60
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.450
and we can choose to allow, drop, or get alerted

61
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.720
on the traffic that will match the rules that we've set up.

62
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.300
And we also have active flow inspections,

63
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.440
so we can actually have an intrusion prevention capability

64
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.480
just like we have with the Gateway Load Balancer,

65
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.940
but all managed by AWS

66
00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.920
and all the rule matches can also

67
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.500
be sent to Amazon S3, CloudWatch Logs

68
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.560
and Kinesis Data Firehose for analysis.

69
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.780
So let's have a look at architectures

70
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.210
using the AWS Network Firewall.

71
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.010
So let's assume we have multiple VPCs

72
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.800
with instances in them, and we want to have all

73
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.950
traffic go through an inspection VPC.

74
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.800
This is where we're going to analyze all our traffic.

75
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.470
On top of it, we have requirements to access the internet.

76
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.370
So we're going to create an egress VPC within that gateway.

77
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.850
An internet gateway, which gives us access to the internet

78
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.330
and we may have also an on-premises data center

79
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.310
or corporate data center

80
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.470
and therefore we need to have a VPN connection,

81
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.150
a Side-to-Side VPN connection

82
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.880
and an AWS Direct Connect Gateway.

83
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.080
So we want to have everything go through the inspection VPC

84
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.240
and in the inspection VPC, we're going

85
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.750
to have the AWS Network Firewall Service.

86
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.300
So to interconnect everything

87
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.720
and make sure your traffic is going to be properly routed,

88
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.700
when you see something this complicated

89
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:45.810
you have to think directly

90
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.990
about the AWS Transit Gateway at the center,

91
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.750
which enables you to do tons of cool patterns.

92
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:52.770
So let's have a look

93
00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.280
at North South VPC egress to the internet.

94
00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.890
That means from the VPC to the internet,

95
00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.320
but through the inspection VPC.

96
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.610
So you can just post right now

97
00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.770
and map it out on here

98
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:05.970
to see how this would work,

99
00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.440
but hopefully you get it right.

100
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.730
So first the traffic goes from the VPC instances

101
00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.403
to the Transit Gateway, and then you create routing

102
00:04:14.403 --> 00:04:17.580
within the Transit Gateway to send everything

103
00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.470
to the inspection VPC,

104
00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.650
which sends back the traffic to the Transit Gateway

105
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.440
which then forwards the traffic to the egress VPC.

106
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.012
And then from there it goes

107
00:04:27.012 --> 00:04:30.240
to the internet through the Internet Gateway.

108
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.230
So that makes sense

109
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.140
and so with this, we send to the Transit Gateway

110
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.550
and the Transit Gateway is responsible

111
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.820
for setting all traffic into the inspection VPC.

112
00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.370
So this is similar if you want to inspect traffic

113
00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.580
before it goes to your on-premises data center.

114
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.260
Again, we need to set the Transit Gateway

115
00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.200
so the traffic goes from the instances

116
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.730
into the Transit Gateway,

117
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.880
which goes into the Network Firewall back and forth.

118
00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.920
And then the Transit Gateway will send it

119
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.550
to your VPN connection or to your Direct Connect Gateway.

120
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.190
And if you want to have East West VPC to VPC,

121
00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.770
so that means from your VPC to another VPC

122
00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.380
but through the inspection VPC, again

123
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.730
it goes from your instances

124
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.260
into the Transit Gateway,

125
00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.090
through the Network Firewall,

126
00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.090
and then finally into your instances.

127
00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.580
So it's a pretty easy architecture once you've seen it once,

128
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.680
but everything goes from the Transit Gateway

129
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.840
to the Network Firewall to get inspected

130
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.520
and then back to the Transit Gateway

131
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.100
to go to its final destination.

132
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:26.933
Okay, that's it.

133
00:05:26.933 --> 00:05:28.050
I hope you liked it and

134
00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.800
I will see you in the next lecture.

